/assets/c4a233bd69566c25e0aeb6736d21dca4aff73a92015a2831d0d0e8fb0243f7ec.png) is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064857/assets/722fa4913d73cae67f202a7a25fec74cdad63866bb4297bf8e8058b4808f4c20.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064857/example-8939.json" "file_sha256" "44a0a094e0386cdea06a37f2488148e5699a31e30aba43831591ea7fdb5b2633"} {"question" "e-8940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle v^2, \\: u-v, \\: u^2 \\big\\rangle$ at the point where $u=2$ and $v=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064857/example-8940.json" "file_sha256" "4b81a7eac7f1506e20b09ef57b4c059a1efb61aadbb211ad8a9b283252f1037a"} {"question" "e-8940" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these vectors when $u=2$ and $v=-1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(2,-1) & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(2,-1) & =⟨2(-1),\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(2,-1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(2,-1) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-8,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parameters $u=2$ and $v=-1$ correspond to the point \n\n$$\n\\big( (-1)^2, \\: 2-(-1), \\: 2^2 \\big) = (1, 3, 4),\n$$\n\nthe equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,\\,-8,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-3,\\,z-4⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4\\cdot (x-1)+(-8)\\cdot (y-3)+2\\cdot (z-4) & =0 \\\\ 4x-8y+2z+12 & =0 \\\\ 2x-4y+z+6 & =0 \\\\ 2x-4y+z & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064857/example-8940.json" "file_sha256" "4b81a7eac7f1506e20b09ef57b4c059a1efb61aadbb211ad8a9b283252f1037a"} {"question" "e-8941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u+v, \\quad\ny=3u^2, \\quad\nz=u-v\n$$\n\nat the point $(2 ,3, 0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064857/example-8941.json" "file_sha256" "948e3c61c4d37aba5d6afc40674485cd94eb71e0265c74940d13f2eba7ce60cb"} {"question" "e-8941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u+v, \\: 3u^2, \\: u-v \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $(2, 3, 0)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064857/assets/9a57281535511312d2fda6f388161fcd624354785d73d48954d10f9e7821653b.png)\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,6u,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =⟨1,\\,6(1),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,6,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,1) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,1) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,\\,2,\\,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-6,\\,2,\\,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-3,\\,z⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-6)\\cdot (x-2)+2\\cdot (y-3)+(-6)\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ -6x+2y-6z+6 & =0 \\\\ -3x+y-3z+3 & =0 \\\\ 3x-y+3z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064857/example-8941.json" "file_sha256" "948e3c61c4d37aba5d6afc40674485cd94eb71e0265c74940d13f2eba7ce60cb"}] "usage" {"e-8938" [] "e-8940" [] "q-105544" [] "q-105594" [] "q-109818" [] "e-8939" [] "q-109888" [] "e-8941" []} "basis" 2125 "retractions" [[17592186172191 :question/problem "Compute a normal vector to the parametric surface defined by the equations\n\n$x = u\\cos v, y = u, z = u\\sin v$\nat the point where $u = 1$ and $v = \\frac{π}{2}$."] [17592186172154 :question/problem "Compute a normal vector to the parametric surface defined by the equations\n\n$x = u + v, y = u^{2}v, z = v$\nat the point where $u = 1$ and $v = 2$."] [17592186172156 :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u, v) = ⟨2u, u\\sin v, 4u^{2}⟩$ at the point where $u = 1$ and $v = π$."] [17592186172190 :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface defined by the equations\n\n$x = u^{2}, y = v^{2}, z = u + 2v$\nat the point $(4, 1, 4)$."] [17592186171799 :question/problem "Compute a normal vector to the parametric surface defined by the equations\n$x=u, \\quad y=1+u^2, \\quad z=v^2$\nat the point where $u=1$ and $v=2$."] [17592186171799 :question/worked-solution "In vector form, our surface has the equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: 1 + u^2, \\: v^2\\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′}(u, v) &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(1 + u^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 2u, 0⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′}(u, v) &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(1 + u^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0, 2v⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′}(1, 2) &= ⟨1, 2(1), 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′}(1, 2) &= ⟨0, 0, 2(2)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0, 4⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point where $u=1$ and $v=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= r_{u}^{′}(1, 2) \\cdot r_{v}^{′}(1, 2) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨8,-4, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186171800 :question/problem "Compute a normal vector to the parametric surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)= \\big\\langle u^2-2, \\: u-v, \\: v^3+1 \\big\\rangle$ at the point $(-1,2,0)$."] [17592186171800 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $(-1,2,0)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n{u^{2} - 2 =-1; u - v = 2; v^{3} + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′}(u, v) &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u^{2} - 2), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u - v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v^{3} + 1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2u, 1, 0⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′}(u, v) &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u^{2} - 2), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u - v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v^{3} + 1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0,-1, 3v^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=-1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′}(1,-1) &= ⟨2(1), 1, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 1, 0⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′}(1,-1) &= ⟨0,-1, 3(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0,-1, 3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point $(3,1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= r_{u}^{′}(1,-1) \\cdot r_{v}^{′}(1,-1) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-6,-2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186171801 :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle v^2, \\: u-v, \\: u^2 \\big\\rangle$ at the point where $u=2$ and $v=-1$."] [17592186171801 :question/worked-solution "We first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′}(u, v) &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u - v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1, 2u⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′}(u, v) &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u - v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2v,-1, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these vectors when $u=2$ and $v=-1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′}(2,-1) &= ⟨0, 1, 2(2)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1, 4⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′}(2,-1) &= ⟨2(-1),-1, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 2,-1, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= r_{u}^{′}(2,-1) \\cdot r_{v}^{′}(2,-1) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨4,-8, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parameters $u=2$ and $v=-1$ correspond to the point\n\n$$\n\\big((-1)^2, \\: 2-(-1), \\: 2^2 \\big) = (1, 3, 4)\n$$\n\nthe equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn \\cdot ⟨x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}, z - z_{0}⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n⟨4,-8, 2⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 3, z - 4⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n4 \\cdot (x - 1) + (-8) \\cdot (y - 3) + 2 \\cdot (z - 4) &= 0 \\\\\n4x - 8y + 2z + 12 &= 0 \\\\\n2x - 4y + z + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n2x - 4y + z &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186171802 :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface defined by the equations\n$x=u+v, \\quad y=3u^2, \\quad z=u-v$\nat the point $(2,3, 0)$."] [17592186171802 :question/worked-solution "In vector form, our surface has the equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u+v, \\: 3u^2, \\: u-v \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $(2, 3, 0)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n{u + v = 2; 3u^{2} = 3; u - v = 0\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′}(u, v) &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u + v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u - v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 6u, 1⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′}(u, v) &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u + v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u - v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 0,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′}(1, 1) &= ⟨1, 6(1), 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 6, 1⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′}(1, 1) &= ⟨1, 0,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= r_{u}^{′}(1, 1) \\cdot r_{v}^{′}(1, 1) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 6, 2,-6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn \\cdot ⟨x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}, z - z_{0}⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n⟨ - 6, 2,-6⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 2, y - 3, z⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n(-6) \\cdot (x - 2) + 2 \\cdot (y - 3) + (-6) \\cdot z &= 0 \\\\\n- 6x + 2y - 6z + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3x + y - 3z + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n3x - y + 3z &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186168128 17592186168129 17592186168130 17592186168131 17592186168132 17592186168140 17592186168141 17592186168142 17592186168143 17592186168144 17592186168344 17592186168345 17592186168346 17592186168347 17592186168348 17592186168350 17592186168351 17592186168352 17592186168353 17592186168354] "immutable_fields" [[17592186168127 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168127 :answer-field/id] [17592186168127 :answer-field/key] [17592186168127 :answer-field/type] [17592186168139 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168139 :answer-field/id] [17592186168139 :answer-field/key] [17592186168139 :answer-field/type] [17592186168343 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168343 :answer-field/id] [17592186168343 :answer-field/key] [17592186168343 :answer-field/type] [17592186168349 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168349 :answer-field/id] [17592186168349 :answer-field/key] [17592186168349 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
